    {
      "beat_id": "B01",
      "act": "PROBLEM — why care",
      "role_note": "the problem, the stakes — BEFORE the CLI",
      "narration_text": "A hospital deploys a sepsis detection model with ninety percent sensitivity and eighty-two percent specificity. The ICU team is told: when it fires, the patient probably has sepsis. But in a population where sepsis occurs in two percent of admissions, the positive predictive value — the chance the alert is real — is under ten percent. Nine out of ten alerts are false. The model is not broken. The base rate is. This video shows why sensitivity and specificity are not enough, and how Bayes' theorem tells you the number that actually matters.",
      "visual_intent": "title card: 'sensitivity=90%, PPV=9%' — the base rate gap; PROBLEM beat, no terminal",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "fade",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "command": "sensitivity=90%, PPV=9% — the base rate gap",
            "topic": "COMPUTATIONAL SKEPTICISM",
            "segment": "Base Rate Neglect, taken apart.",
            "greeting": "",
            "runningText": "framing the problem…",
            "output": [],
            "folderLabel": "@NikBearBrown",
            "modelLabel": "Fable 5",
            "effortLabel": "High"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B01.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 16,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B01.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 29.7,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B01.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-19T16:44:14"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "act": "ASK",
      "role_note": "terminal (Onda) — the command typed",
      "narration_text": "In the terminal: ask Claude to write a Bayes PPV function, then compute a five-by-five grid over prevalence and sensitivity at a fixed specificity of point-eight-two — the sepsis model's published value — and mark every cell where PPV drops below ten percent.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "fade",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "command": "claude \"Write bayes_ppv(prevalence, sensitivity, specificity)\nusing Bayes' theorem. Compute 5x5 grid:\n  prevalence=[0.005,0.01,0.02,0.05,0.10]\n  sensitivity=[0.70,0.80,0.85,0.90,0.99]\n  specificity=0.82\nPrint as pandas DataFrame.\nMark cells where PPV < 0.10.\"",
            "topic": "COMPUTATIONAL SKEPTICISM — BASE-RATE NEGLECT",
            "segment": "BASE-RATE BAYES CALCULATOR",
            "greeting": "",
            "runningText": "generating Bayes PPV grid…",
            "output": [],
            "folderLabel": "@NikBearBrown",
            "modelLabel": "Fable 5",
            "effortLabel": "High"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B02.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 14,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 14.91,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-19T16:44:14"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "act": "CODE",
      "role_note": "terminal (Onda) — read the code before running",
      "narration_text": "Read the key formula before running. PPV equals sensitivity times prevalence, divided by sensitivity times prevalence plus one-minus-specificity times one-minus-prevalence. The denominator is where the base rate lives. When prevalence is point-zero-zero-five — half a percent — that denominator is dominated by the false positive rate, and sensitivity can't save you.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "fade",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeCodeBeat",
          "props": {
            "title": "bayes_ppv.py",
            "code": "# bayes_ppv.py  —  Claude Code output\n\ndef bayes_ppv(prevalence, sensitivity, specificity):\n    \"\"\"Positive Predictive Value via Bayes' theorem.\"\"\"\n    tp = sensitivity * prevalence\n    fp = (1 - specificity) * (1 - prevalence)\n    ppv = tp / (tp + fp)\n    return ppv\n\n# At prevalence=0.02, sensitivity=0.90, specificity=0.82:\n# ppv = (0.90 * 0.02) / (0.90*0.02 + 0.18*0.98)\n#     = 0.018 / (0.018 + 0.1764)\n#     = 0.018 / 0.1944  ≈ 0.093  <- under 10%",
            "sparkLine": "BASE-RATE BAYES CALCULATOR"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B03.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 14,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 20.91,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-19T16:44:14"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "act": "OUTPUT — run",
      "role_note": "Manim animation: B04_BayesHeatmap",
      "narration_text": "Run it. The heatmap shows twenty-five cells: prevalence on rows, sensitivity on columns. The top two rows — prevalence under one percent — are entirely crimson. Every cell is below ten percent PPV, regardless of how high sensitivity goes. The gold band in rows three and four is the transition zone. Only when prevalence reaches ten percent do you start seeing green — and even then, PPV tops out at thirty-five percent with ninety-nine percent sensitivity.",
      "visual_intent": "Manim B04_BayesHeatmap: 5x5 PPV grid colored crimson/gold/green by threshold; sepsis operating point highlighted; 'PPV < 10% zone' annotation on top 2 rows",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "manim",
        "motion": "fade",
        "manim": {
          "scene_class": "B04_BayesHeatmap",
          "file": "vox_scenes.py"
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 17,
